WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:01.590
MIKE COLEMAN: Hi.

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:05.190
I'm Mike Coleman, a developer
advocate at Google Cloud.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.710
Many years back my friend
Keith and I were replacing

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.690
a blown head gasket on my car.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.000
I went to remove a bolt
with an adjustable wrench.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.250
And Keith stopped me
cold in my tracks.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.470
He then said something
that has stuck with me ever

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.225
since, "Always use the
right tool for the job,"

00:00:20.225 --> 00:00:21.600
as he handed me
a inappropriately

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.580
sized box wrench.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.620
We are seeing this
advice play out today

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.110
in the way organizations
are leveraging the cloud.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:29.940
They are choosing
different cloud providers

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.610
for different workloads
based on a variety of factors

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.040
in an effort to ensure they
are using the right tool

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.390
for the job at hand.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.120
However, using multiple
cloud providers

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.940
comes with added complexity in
the form of multiple management

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.430
interfaces, disparate
policy enforcement,

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.770
and complex security
implications.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.690
In this video, I
want to talk a bit

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.060
about how Anthos can help
reduce the complexity

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.190
around multicloud
Kubernetes deployments

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:57.164
with a very specific focus
on Microsoft's Azure.

00:00:57.164 --> 00:01:00.468
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.519
Before we move into
Anthos on Azure,

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:08.370
let's talk about Anthos
in more general terms.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.680
Anthos provides a
unified platform

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.950
for managing Kubernetes clusters
across different clouds,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.390
as well as on prem.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:16.920
These clusters can
be running inside

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.690
of virtual machines
and instances,

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:21.060
as well as on bare metal.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.300
Anthos is comprised of an
integrated set of services

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.230
developed to ensure the entire
IT organization-- operators,

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:29.970
developers, security teams--

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.570
can accomplish their duties
without a siloed approach.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.360
Anthos is made up of several
different components,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.850
such as container management.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.100
Anthos provides a
reliable and efficient way

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.500
to deploy and run containers.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.580
Service management, Anthos
features a managed service mesh

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.030
offering to connect, manage
and secure VMs and containers.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.300
Policy management,
Anthos Config Management

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:56.920
provides a central
place to manage

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.050
configuration and
security policies

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.480
across your entire fleet.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.970
Operations management,
Anthos is integrated

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:06.980
with cloud logging
and cloud monitoring

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.199
to provide an integrated
logging and monitoring

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:13.950
service for containers,
services, and application.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.440
Specific to multicloud,
Anthos allows

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:21.000
you to manage clusters on
Google Cloud, AWS, and Azure.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.390
Now let's dive a little
deeper into Anthos on Azure.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.580
When you deploy an
Anthos on Azure cluster,

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.310
you end up running a
compliant Kubernetes cluster

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.100
on Azure native resources.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.280
This cluster can be accessed
via the Cloud Console,

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.510
gcloud command line, or kubectl,
just like any other Kubernetes

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.530
cluster.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.110
Additionally, as we'll
see in the demo later,

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.690
the cluster will show up
alongside clusters provisioned

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.080
in other environments,
such as Google Cloud, AWS,

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.140
or bare metal inside of
the Google Cloud console.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.720
The Anthos on Azure
architecture is comprised

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.020
of several components.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.780
The first is a
multicloud cluster API.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.840
This is a standard
Google Cloud REST

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.840
API, which provides
cluster CRUD operations.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.820
Several Azure
specific parameters

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:10.180
are passed, such as the
subscription ID, the VNet ID,

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.540
and the subnet ID, along with
parameters for the cluster size

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.070
and configuration.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.460
Next, we have the multicloud
cluster controller.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:22.270
When a cluster is created using
the Anthos multicloud API,

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:25.360
a long running operation
is created with the Google

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.430
Cloud-based cluster controller.

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.710
This controller will
create the cluster in Azure

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:30.935
asynchronously.

00:03:30.935 --> 00:03:32.560
You can check the
status of the cluster

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.470
operation via a Get-Cluster
call to the API.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:39.340
Next, we have Anthos
service account.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.790
Once the Anthos multicloud API
is enabled on your Google Cloud

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.340
project, a Google
managed service account

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.250
will be automatically
created within your project.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:51.220
This service account will
be the security principle

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:54.550
calling the Azure APIs.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.640
Additionally, we have the
Anthos app registration.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.470
In order for the
cluster controller

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:02.230
to create Azure resources
within your subscription using

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:05.650
the Anthos service account, an
Azure application registration

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:09.130
must be created within the
Azure Active Directory.

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:11.890
Finally, there's
the Connect API.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.410
In order to integrate into
the Google Cloud console,

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.420
a Connect agent
running on the cluster

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:21.399
makes an outbound TLS
call to the Connect API.

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:24.160
In any cloud environment there
are multiple different types

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:25.150
of resources.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:27.110
Deploying Anthos
is no different.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.590
However, because Anthos
automates the lifecycle

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.590
of the cluster, some resources
are managed by Anthos,

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.382
while others are managed by you.

00:04:34.382 --> 00:04:36.340
Well, now that you have
an understanding of how

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:39.010
Anthos on Azure clusters
are built and managed,

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.630
let's take a look
at a quick demo

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:44.290
to give you a firsthand look
at how it all comes together.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:45.942
The first thing
I'm going to do is

00:04:45.942 --> 00:04:47.650
create what we're
calling a client, which

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:50.860
is a set of credentials
that we can use to operate

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:52.670
against our Azure resources.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:54.460
So I've created that client.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.972
Now I'm going to go ahead
and export the certificate.

00:04:56.972 --> 00:04:58.430
I'm going to take
that certificate.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:00.790
And I'm going to
upload it into Azure.

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:03.135
So let's move into
the Azure console.

00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:04.510
And I'll come over
here, and I'll

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.730
click on Certificates
and Secrets.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:08.395
And then I'll do
Upload Certificate.

00:05:08.395 --> 00:05:10.645
And I'll choose that certificate
that we just created.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:14.220
Come down here.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:15.630
I'll click the Add button.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:17.460
And I now have that
certificate uploaded.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:20.477
And I can authenticate
against those Azure resources.

00:05:20.477 --> 00:05:22.810
Now let's go ahead and create
our control plane cluster.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.170
You'll notice here that I'm
actually specifying things

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:29.460
like the Azure subnet ID, the VM
size that's specific to Azure,

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.838
so a bunch of Azure
specific parameters.

00:05:31.838 --> 00:05:33.880
After a few minutes that
cluster will be created.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:35.172
So let's wait for it to finish.

00:05:35.172 --> 00:05:35.743
There it is.

00:05:35.743 --> 00:05:37.660
So we've got the control
plane up and running.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:41.250
Now let's go ahead
and add the node pool.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:44.200
So a lot like the last
command, when we issue this,

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.980
we'll put in some Azure
specific parameters

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.310
to create that node pool.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:49.935
So we'll give that
a couple of minutes.

00:05:49.935 --> 00:05:52.080
Fast forward the video,
and that node pool

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.693
will be created as well.

00:05:54.693 --> 00:05:56.360
Now, if we move up
to the Azure console,

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.010
we can actually see
all those resources

00:05:58.010 --> 00:05:59.990
we just created using gcloud.

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:02.390
So using the Google command
line, we were able to go in

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:06.680
and provision things like
NICs, and VMs, IP addresses,

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.260
load balancers, all of
those Azure resources.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:12.170
Now, if we want the cluster
to show up in Google Cloud,

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:15.512
we need to create
a hub membership.

00:06:15.512 --> 00:06:16.970
So we'll create
the hub membership.

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:19.430
And we'll install the Connect
agent on that cluster.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:22.590
And that will allow it to show
back up in the Google Cloud

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:23.090
console.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.350
So if I move over there,
there's our cluster.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:27.410
So if I click on the
name, you can get details

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:29.110
about the cluster.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:30.860
We can go up, and then
click on the nodes.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:33.470
We can explore
the details there.

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:36.650
And again, these
are the same VMs

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:38.420
that we saw running
in the Azure console.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:41.360
And if I come up to storage,
you can actually even see

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:43.130
the names of those disks.

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:46.310
And you can see like,
disk.csi.azure.com.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:47.853
And those are Azure resources.

00:06:47.853 --> 00:06:49.520
Now, I don't have any
workloads running,

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.480
just that Connect agent
I installed earlier.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:56.040
So let me do a quick deployment
of a microservices demo.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:58.310
So we'll do the Kubernetes
deployment YAML here,

00:06:58.310 --> 00:06:59.630
create a bunch of services.

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:00.440
We'll move back.

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:01.905
We'll refresh our workloads.

00:07:01.905 --> 00:07:03.530
Notice how we're
using the Google Cloud

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:06.140
console to look at the
workloads running up in Azure.

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:08.040
So that's that
single pane of glass.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.100
So they're not all running yet.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:11.060
Refresh.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:14.000
Give it a second, and
everything's up and running.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.860
Now, if I move over
here and I click

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.290
on the left on
Services &amp; Ingress,

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:21.740
I can find the IP address of
the Azure base load balancer.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:22.827
And I can click on that.

00:07:22.827 --> 00:07:24.410
And we'll see our
application running.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:25.520
So we'll click here.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.490
There's the application
running on Kubernetes, deployed

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.380
from Google Cloud into Azure.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:31.380
Well, there you have it.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:32.990
That's Anthos on Azure.

